WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.227 --> 00:00:02.077
- Hello, Sal here from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.077 --> 00:00:04.297
Welcome to our Homeroom live stream,

00:00:04.297 --> 00:00:06.757
I have a very exciting guest today.

00:00:06.757 --> 00:00:08.697
But before we jump into that conversation,

00:00:08.697 --> 00:00:11.787
I will give my standard
reminders to folks, one,

00:00:11.787 --> 00:00:14.287
just reminder that we are a
not-for-profit organization,

00:00:14.287 --> 00:00:16.937
the work that Khan Academy
does is only possible

00:00:16.937 --> 00:00:20.117
because of philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself.

00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:21.407
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:21.407 --> 00:00:23.244
please think about making a donation.

00:00:23.244 --> 00:00:26.367
You can go to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:27.987
Also wanna make a special shout out

00:00:27.987 --> 00:00:30.647
to several organizations
that when they realized

00:00:30.647 --> 00:00:32.817
that we were already running
at a deficit pre-COVID,

00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:35.577
and in order to support, frankly the world

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:37.867
through the school closures,

00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:39.737
we were running an even a larger deficit.

00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:42.377
Special thanks to Bank of
America, Google.org, AT&amp;T,

00:00:42.377 --> 00:00:45.127
Fastly, and Novartis,
and the Amgen Foundation

00:00:45.127 --> 00:00:46.937
for stepping up and
helping us close that gap.

00:00:46.937 --> 00:00:48.257
Although we are still running a gap,

00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:50.887
so any support at any
level for Khan Academy,

00:00:50.887 --> 00:00:52.237
if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:52.237 --> 00:00:53.847
it allows for a lot of other folks

00:00:53.847 --> 00:00:56.137
who aren't in the position to do

00:00:56.137 --> 00:00:57.807
to continue to keep learning,

00:00:57.807 --> 00:00:58.897
especially as we're going through

00:00:58.897 --> 00:01:01.287
these very, very hard times.

00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:05.657
So with that, I'm super
excited to introduce our guest,

00:01:05.657 --> 00:01:07.747
Maria Elena Salinas.

00:01:07.747 --> 00:01:10.537
For many of y'all, she
is probably an icon,

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:13.307
someone that you grew up
with, for some of y'all,

00:01:13.307 --> 00:01:15.627
she might be a relatively new name,

00:01:15.627 --> 00:01:17.187
but for those of y'all say that

00:01:17.187 --> 00:01:19.397
she is an icon for many poor folks.

00:01:19.397 --> 00:01:21.627
She anchored Univision for many decades,

00:01:21.627 --> 00:01:23.227
she's a contributor to CBS News,

00:01:23.227 --> 00:01:27.347
really one of the iconic
journalists in the world.

00:01:27.347 --> 00:01:30.230
Maria, thanks so much for joining us.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:33.287
- Thank you so much Sal for
that very kind introduction

00:01:33.287 --> 00:01:37.567
and (speaking in foreign
language) that's hello

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:40.387
to those of us who are
listening in Spanish

00:01:40.387 --> 00:01:41.220
or watching in Spanish.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.597
- Yeah, and thanks for that.

00:01:42.597 --> 00:01:45.178
'Cause today we do have
our Spanish speaking.

00:01:45.178 --> 00:01:49.257
There's a fully Spanish
version of Khan Academy,

00:01:49.257 --> 00:01:51.837
es.khanacademy.org and
we're also posting this

00:01:51.837 --> 00:01:55.917
on our Spanish language sites,
so yes, excited to have it-

00:01:55.917 --> 00:01:58.090
- I notice there khan co
(speaking in foreign language),

00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:01.667
that's really good.
Let's continue learning.

00:02:02.697 --> 00:02:03.530
- Exactly,

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:04.887
- I can see it on the screen.

00:02:06.277 --> 00:02:08.277
- So there's two things I
really wanna talk about today.

00:02:08.277 --> 00:02:11.317
Obviously we're having this conversation

00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:14.337
in very suboptimal times,
we're going through a pandemic

00:02:14.337 --> 00:02:18.087
the pandemic has health impact,
it has an economic impact,

00:02:18.087 --> 00:02:19.647
it's having an education impact,

00:02:19.647 --> 00:02:24.247
and it does look like it's
disproportionately affecting

00:02:24.247 --> 00:02:27.217
the Latinx community,
especially in the United States.

00:02:27.217 --> 00:02:28.257
I definitely wanna talk about that,

00:02:28.257 --> 00:02:30.317
and I also wanna make sure
we have time to talk about

00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:32.217
your journey, which I find very inspiring

00:02:32.217 --> 00:02:34.387
and I think a lot of young people watching

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:36.337
will find inspiring as
well, but let's start,

00:02:36.337 --> 00:02:39.437
I guess maybe on the world
that we're in right now,

00:02:39.437 --> 00:02:42.847
you recently did a
documentary on the pandemic

00:02:42.847 --> 00:02:46.467
and how it's affecting the
Latinx community in particular.

00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:48.967
What were the big headlines from that?

00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:50.077
What were your big takeaways?

00:02:50.077 --> 00:02:52.277
What did you learn from that?

00:02:52.277 --> 00:02:53.110
- Well, for one thing,

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:56.387
I'm very happy that CBS
decided to do this special

00:02:56.387 --> 00:02:59.207
lot of thing, it was enough to do just to,

00:02:59.207 --> 00:03:01.987
one, two minutes, three minutes
stories in their newscasts

00:03:01.987 --> 00:03:04.708
in their regularly scheduled programs,

00:03:04.708 --> 00:03:07.347
but it was important and
it was important also

00:03:07.347 --> 00:03:09.587
that we had a full Latino staff

00:03:09.587 --> 00:03:13.297
all the reporters, producers,
researchers, bookers,

00:03:13.297 --> 00:03:17.487
were all Latino, because
there's a relativity

00:03:17.487 --> 00:03:19.537
that we have to the subject
and to the community.

00:03:19.537 --> 00:03:22.347
We know our community
better than anyone else.

00:03:22.347 --> 00:03:25.417
And I think the big
headlines out of that really

00:03:25.417 --> 00:03:28.157
is that you said it, we
are disproportionately

00:03:28.157 --> 00:03:30.487
being affected by this pandemic.

00:03:30.487 --> 00:03:33.097
Latinos are getting
sick in higher numbers,

00:03:33.097 --> 00:03:35.627
Latinos are dying in higher numbers,

00:03:35.627 --> 00:03:38.257
Latinos are losing their
jobs in higher numbers

00:03:38.257 --> 00:03:40.867
or making less money.

00:03:40.867 --> 00:03:44.937
They are the essential workers
that keep the economy going

00:03:44.937 --> 00:03:49.937
and while they do have a job
they're risking their lives,

00:03:50.357 --> 00:03:53.627
and many of them don't
qualify for unemployment.

00:03:53.627 --> 00:03:57.207
They don't qualify for a stimulus cheque.

00:03:57.207 --> 00:03:59.957
So they're being affected
I think in economically

00:04:01.082 --> 00:04:06.082
and also health wise, they're
being very, very effected

00:04:06.327 --> 00:04:07.277
and it's not getting better,

00:04:07.277 --> 00:04:09.677
it's getting worse, unfortunately.

00:04:09.677 --> 00:04:11.827
- And what do you think
are the main levers

00:04:11.827 --> 00:04:13.617
that are causing this inequity?

00:04:13.617 --> 00:04:15.587
And then what do you think can be done

00:04:15.587 --> 00:04:19.170
to at least lessen hopefully
eliminate some of these issues?

00:04:20.867 --> 00:04:22.957
- Well, it's the historical inequities

00:04:22.957 --> 00:04:27.327
that have been really
exacerbated by the pandemic

00:04:27.327 --> 00:04:32.327
poverty, unemployment,
insufficient savings.

00:04:35.577 --> 00:04:37.627
I think there was one study that said

00:04:37.627 --> 00:04:41.187
that more than half of
Latinos had only $500 or less

00:04:41.187 --> 00:04:45.397
in their savings, lack of
access to adequate healthcare,

00:04:45.397 --> 00:04:48.870
all these traditional inequities
or historical inequities,

00:04:49.885 --> 00:04:52.027
they just got worse during this time.

00:04:52.027 --> 00:04:53.837
And when it comes to education,

00:04:53.837 --> 00:04:56.007
a subject that I know that
you're passionate about,

00:04:56.007 --> 00:04:59.500
and that has to become your
mission and your passion,

00:05:00.357 --> 00:05:04.047
Latino kids were staying
behind more than others,

00:05:04.047 --> 00:05:05.177
more than any others.

00:05:05.177 --> 00:05:08.887
And that is because only one
in three Latino households

00:05:08.887 --> 00:05:11.937
has access to internet
and only half of them,

00:05:11.937 --> 00:05:15.247
about 50% have enough computers at home,

00:05:15.247 --> 00:05:16.127
not saying they don't have computers,

00:05:16.127 --> 00:05:18.437
they don't have enough computers.

00:05:18.437 --> 00:05:21.157
The majority of essential
workers do the types of jobs

00:05:21.157 --> 00:05:24.007
that you can't do at home
from the comfort of your home,

00:05:24.007 --> 00:05:26.821
in a Zoom Box, so they
do have go out and work,

00:05:26.821 --> 00:05:29.047
but the ones that do have
to rely on the computer

00:05:29.047 --> 00:05:31.727
and if they have two or three
kids, they don't have enough.

00:05:31.727 --> 00:05:35.497
And even though school
districts across the country

00:05:35.497 --> 00:05:39.337
have been distributing computers, laptops,

00:05:39.337 --> 00:05:41.727
or tablets to kids around the country,

00:05:41.727 --> 00:05:43.077
it hasn't been enough.

00:05:43.077 --> 00:05:45.408
This morning, there was an
article in the Los Angeles Times

00:05:45.408 --> 00:05:48.237
that talk precisely
about that point and said

00:05:48.237 --> 00:05:50.487
that in California alone,

00:05:50.487 --> 00:05:54.727
kids need more than 1 million computers

00:05:54.727 --> 00:05:58.230
to be able to go back to
school and study from home.

00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:01.377
- Now this last point,
obviously, as you know,

00:06:01.377 --> 00:06:03.007
this has been something
that we've at Khan Academy,

00:06:03.007 --> 00:06:05.507
we've been very focused on
throughout the pandemic,

00:06:05.507 --> 00:06:08.650
even pre-pandemic around
the digital divide.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.037
There's the education issue,
there's the economic issue,

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:12.857
and then there's the healthcare issue.

00:06:12.857 --> 00:06:14.987
And maybe we'll start
with the education issue

00:06:14.987 --> 00:06:17.987
just where you left off,
what do you think we can do?

00:06:17.987 --> 00:06:19.247
Because I'm seeing the same thing,

00:06:19.247 --> 00:06:22.317
when we talk to district
officials, we talked to educators,

00:06:22.317 --> 00:06:24.197
even where there have been heroic efforts

00:06:24.197 --> 00:06:25.917
to get a lot of laptops out there.

00:06:25.917 --> 00:06:27.277
They're still losing a lot of kids,

00:06:27.277 --> 00:06:30.337
and these kids are
disproportionately students of color.

00:06:30.337 --> 00:06:32.247
A lot of their parents, as you mentioned,

00:06:32.247 --> 00:06:33.607
they might be essential workers.

00:06:33.607 --> 00:06:34.837
They're the folks that are making sure

00:06:34.837 --> 00:06:35.997
that we can get our groceries,

00:06:35.997 --> 00:06:37.727
they're the folks that are making sure

00:06:37.727 --> 00:06:39.387
that other things are up and running.

00:06:39.387 --> 00:06:42.247
And so these kids are less supported,

00:06:42.247 --> 00:06:45.587
but personally I've been
at a loss of knowing

00:06:45.587 --> 00:06:48.947
what the solution is, what can we do?

00:06:48.947 --> 00:06:51.107
Obviously on our level, we're
trying as much as possible,

00:06:51.107 --> 00:06:53.617
Hey, Khan Academy is there,
if you have internet access,

00:06:53.617 --> 00:06:56.177
if you have a device,
even a cell phone use us,

00:06:56.177 --> 00:06:58.677
that's something, but what else can we do?

00:06:58.677 --> 00:07:00.427
Should we think about as a society.

00:07:01.727 --> 00:07:02.647
- Well, you're right.

00:07:02.647 --> 00:07:04.467
Latina parents are very concerned.

00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:09.057
In fact, a recent survey
by Latino decisions

00:07:09.057 --> 00:07:13.097
that focuses only on Latino
voters or Latino families

00:07:13.097 --> 00:07:15.977
says that 53% are really
thinking of not sending

00:07:15.977 --> 00:07:17.527
their kids back to school.

00:07:17.527 --> 00:07:19.647
Now, a great majority say

00:07:19.647 --> 00:07:21.137
that they wish they
could go back to school,

00:07:21.137 --> 00:07:23.777
but that the only way that
they would send them back,

00:07:23.777 --> 00:07:25.837
is if there were precautions

00:07:25.837 --> 00:07:28.097
such as making sure that
the classroom is clean

00:07:28.097 --> 00:07:31.857
and in such as making sure
that they're wearing masks

00:07:31.857 --> 00:07:35.337
and taking the necessary
precautions there,

00:07:35.337 --> 00:07:39.917
and 83% are concerned that their kids

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:43.367
will be left behind in their studies.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:44.937
And that is a real concern.

00:07:44.937 --> 00:07:47.127
But you know, when you think about kids

00:07:47.127 --> 00:07:49.727
and going back to school and wearing masks

00:07:49.727 --> 00:07:51.887
and taking all the necessary precautions,

00:07:51.887 --> 00:07:54.627
I mean, I see it all the time.

00:07:54.627 --> 00:07:56.627
I have two daughters and
I have two stepdaughters.

00:07:56.627 --> 00:07:58.967
My two stepdaughters are both teachers.

00:07:58.967 --> 00:08:00.147
One of them teaches second grade

00:08:00.147 --> 00:08:02.557
and the other one teaches
at the university level.

00:08:02.557 --> 00:08:06.627
The one that teaches
second grade tells me,

00:08:06.627 --> 00:08:09.737
you sit in a class and not to have kids

00:08:09.737 --> 00:08:11.377
on top of each other, hitting each other,

00:08:11.377 --> 00:08:14.357
teasing each other, or having them wear

00:08:14.357 --> 00:08:16.887
to make sure that the
mask is over their nose,

00:08:16.887 --> 00:08:19.727
it's virtually impossible to do.

00:08:19.727 --> 00:08:22.707
Now, she is only 32 years old.

00:08:22.707 --> 00:08:25.227
She might not be at very high risk,

00:08:25.227 --> 00:08:28.027
but her husband has diabetes.

00:08:28.027 --> 00:08:30.337
He has childhood diabetes.

00:08:30.337 --> 00:08:34.567
If she gets sick and takes
that home, her husband can die.

00:08:34.567 --> 00:08:37.774
So these are the things
that we need to think about.

00:08:37.774 --> 00:08:40.427
And my other stepdaughter who teaches

00:08:40.427 --> 00:08:43.290
at the university level, she
teaches journalism at UCF,

00:08:44.537 --> 00:08:47.227
same thing, they are doing virtual classes

00:08:47.227 --> 00:08:50.417
and she'll be going in
to teach once a week.

00:08:50.417 --> 00:08:53.717
However, she has a seven year
old son who's in second grade

00:08:53.717 --> 00:08:55.747
and he's doing virtual classes says

00:08:55.747 --> 00:08:59.227
she will not let him go to school

00:08:59.227 --> 00:09:02.697
because she's very afraid
that he would get sick

00:09:02.697 --> 00:09:04.617
because she has a little baby.

00:09:04.617 --> 00:09:06.947
So every family has their story.

00:09:06.947 --> 00:09:09.547
Every family has a set of circumstances

00:09:09.547 --> 00:09:12.827
that makes them more vulnerable.

00:09:12.827 --> 00:09:15.187
What the solution would be, of course,

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:18.377
for everyone to have access
to the necessary technology

00:09:18.377 --> 00:09:20.787
so that they can study from home

00:09:21.638 --> 00:09:23.987
so that the kids can
continue learning from home.

00:09:23.987 --> 00:09:25.647
And maybe some of those school districts

00:09:25.647 --> 00:09:29.147
who don't have cases
that are that serious,

00:09:29.147 --> 00:09:31.627
maybe begin going back little by little

00:09:31.627 --> 00:09:33.447
with taking all of these precautions,

00:09:33.447 --> 00:09:35.627
but it really is scary how this has turned

00:09:35.627 --> 00:09:37.157
into a political issue.

00:09:37.157 --> 00:09:39.457
This isn't a matter of red and blue.

00:09:39.457 --> 00:09:41.287
This is a matter of life and death.

00:09:41.287 --> 00:09:46.187
So it's really sad and it's dangerous also

00:09:46.187 --> 00:09:47.517
for this to happen.

00:09:47.517 --> 00:09:51.317
I live in Miami and in Florida,

00:09:51.317 --> 00:09:55.877
the governor is forcing
schools to go back in person

00:09:55.877 --> 00:09:59.837
to have in person classes
otherwise he threatens with

00:09:59.837 --> 00:10:02.007
withholding state funds.

00:10:02.007 --> 00:10:03.657
Now, when the President said

00:10:03.657 --> 00:10:06.017
he would withhold federal funds,

00:10:06.017 --> 00:10:08.567
while you think it's only maybe
five, 10% of their budget,

00:10:08.567 --> 00:10:10.697
but the state funds are a little bit more.

00:10:10.697 --> 00:10:14.320
So it would be detrimental
to school districts.

00:10:15.317 --> 00:10:17.217
- Yeah, I think there's several
things you're touching on

00:10:17.217 --> 00:10:18.677
there, one is a school opening issue,

00:10:18.677 --> 00:10:22.487
and yesterday we had the head of the NEA,

00:10:22.487 --> 00:10:23.597
the largest teachers union,

00:10:23.597 --> 00:10:26.007
and last week we had the head of the NFD,

00:10:26.007 --> 00:10:29.567
and this is one of those
situations where regardless of

00:10:29.567 --> 00:10:32.147
sometimes these things in
education turned very political,

00:10:32.147 --> 00:10:34.057
but this is just a basic
health and safety issue.

00:10:34.057 --> 00:10:37.650
And any epidemiologists that
you talk to will tell you

00:10:38.487 --> 00:10:41.157
if there's a high incidence
in a community right now

00:10:41.157 --> 00:10:44.997
to put kids, put 30 kids in a
not so well ventilated place

00:10:44.997 --> 00:10:48.747
and a teacher who may or may
not be at high risk themselves,

00:10:48.747 --> 00:10:49.887
but they're gonna have family members

00:10:49.887 --> 00:10:51.267
that could be at high risk.

00:10:51.267 --> 00:10:53.587
It's actually just not
even good for those people

00:10:53.587 --> 00:10:55.477
directly involved, it's
bad for the whole community

00:10:55.477 --> 00:10:57.677
because a pandemic, you have to keep,

00:10:57.677 --> 00:11:01.047
it's gonna hurt all of us who
aren't even going to school

00:11:01.047 --> 00:11:03.287
because it'll just create
more spread in the community.

00:11:03.287 --> 00:11:05.917
What, I've seen some good examples,

00:11:05.917 --> 00:11:07.787
actually the head of the
NEA yesterday talked about

00:11:07.787 --> 00:11:11.147
Maryland doing a good job of
where even where kids don't,

00:11:11.147 --> 00:11:13.227
they're going to distance learning,

00:11:13.227 --> 00:11:15.977
which seems appropriate given
where we are in the pandemic.

00:11:15.977 --> 00:11:18.977
But for those kids who need
those extra supports at home,

00:11:18.977 --> 00:11:21.317
who might, maybe their
parent is an essential worker

00:11:21.317 --> 00:11:23.877
and can't support kid while
they're during the school day,

00:11:23.877 --> 00:11:25.907
they are opening the
schools up for a few of them

00:11:25.907 --> 00:11:27.307
in a very careful way,

00:11:27.307 --> 00:11:28.957
but they're still doing
distance learning from school,

00:11:28.957 --> 00:11:31.477
but they're getting some kinda childcare

00:11:31.477 --> 00:11:34.147
and they're getting some
support from some adults.

00:11:34.147 --> 00:11:37.587
Are you seeing programs
that you think are helping,

00:11:37.587 --> 00:11:38.937
I would say, especially
the Latinx community,

00:11:38.937 --> 00:11:40.737
but just in general communities

00:11:40.737 --> 00:11:42.387
that need extra supports right now,

00:11:42.387 --> 00:11:45.537
or are you kind of discouraged by

00:11:45.537 --> 00:11:47.087
the state of affairs right now?

00:11:48.057 --> 00:11:51.867
- I do see a lot of help
and in lieu of having

00:11:51.867 --> 00:11:55.147
our governments at all
levels help these kids.

00:11:55.147 --> 00:11:59.047
You do see philanthropist
stepping up and helping

00:12:00.247 --> 00:12:03.367
with different programs and
helping families with food,

00:12:03.367 --> 00:12:06.147
helping families maybe pay their
rent and also in this case,

00:12:06.147 --> 00:12:09.397
helping families distribute computers.

00:12:09.397 --> 00:12:11.957
People are very kind and
they always tried to help.

00:12:11.957 --> 00:12:14.187
We shouldn't underestimate the kindness

00:12:14.187 --> 00:12:15.437
of the American people.

00:12:15.437 --> 00:12:17.877
There was one case of a family

00:12:17.877 --> 00:12:21.497
that I covered also for
CBS earlier this year,

00:12:21.497 --> 00:12:23.267
just a couple of months ago

00:12:23.267 --> 00:12:25.837
where these three little
boys, the Hernandez boys,

00:12:25.837 --> 00:12:28.787
they had only two computers,

00:12:28.787 --> 00:12:31.647
one that was provided by the school,

00:12:31.647 --> 00:12:33.197
another one that they had at home,

00:12:33.197 --> 00:12:35.427
and then the third boy
who was eight years old,

00:12:35.427 --> 00:12:38.087
had to do his homework
on his father's phone.

00:12:38.087 --> 00:12:41.077
And that created a problem only
because it's very difficult

00:12:41.077 --> 00:12:44.337
for you to do homework on a phone.

00:12:44.337 --> 00:12:47.197
Also not to mention the fact that

00:12:47.197 --> 00:12:50.647
the data would be more
expensive for this family.

00:12:50.647 --> 00:12:52.747
When the father is an unemployed

00:12:52.747 --> 00:12:55.954
because he lost his job because of COVID

00:12:55.954 --> 00:12:58.107
and the mother is a housewife,

00:12:58.107 --> 00:13:00.497
she stays home taking care of the kids.

00:13:00.497 --> 00:13:02.570
So when we aired this story,

00:13:03.427 --> 00:13:06.427
I have so many tweets of people wanting,

00:13:06.427 --> 00:13:09.327
stepping up and wanting to
help and wanting to donate

00:13:09.327 --> 00:13:11.537
a computer to these kids and finally,

00:13:11.537 --> 00:13:14.147
one person did get in
touch with the family

00:13:14.147 --> 00:13:17.827
and did sent the little
boy an extra laptop.

00:13:17.827 --> 00:13:21.057
But we have to rely on the
kindness of our neighbors

00:13:21.057 --> 00:13:25.357
of organizations so that we can have this,

00:13:25.357 --> 00:13:28.427
but let's say that we get
enough computers out there

00:13:28.427 --> 00:13:29.834
that we'll get enough laptops

00:13:29.834 --> 00:13:31.457
and that some philanthropist says,

00:13:31.457 --> 00:13:34.247
okay, we need 1 million
in LA and in California,

00:13:34.247 --> 00:13:35.707
we're gonna give them 1 million.

00:13:35.707 --> 00:13:40.707
There's also the issue
of having access to WiFi.

00:13:40.787 --> 00:13:43.437
In Texas for example, there was one area

00:13:43.437 --> 00:13:46.187
where there was a school
bus that was retrofitted

00:13:46.187 --> 00:13:48.877
to become a WiFi hotspot,

00:13:48.877 --> 00:13:52.977
going to communities where
they knew that these families,

00:13:52.977 --> 00:13:56.047
these low income families
didn't have access to it.

00:13:56.047 --> 00:13:57.907
There were some families that told us

00:13:57.907 --> 00:13:59.837
that they have to get in the car

00:13:59.837 --> 00:14:02.247
and drive to the parking
lot of a McDonald's

00:14:02.247 --> 00:14:04.417
or a Walmart or another store,

00:14:04.417 --> 00:14:07.107
so that they're able
to use those hotspots.

00:14:07.107 --> 00:14:09.820
That is a serious issue,
that's a serious problem.

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:11.840
And I don't know what the solution is.

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:13.087
I mean, is it money?

00:14:13.087 --> 00:14:15.617
Is it always that we
throw money into things

00:14:15.617 --> 00:14:19.407
and then they get solved, money does help,

00:14:19.407 --> 00:14:22.787
but in this case it's gonna
be, it takes a lot more.

00:14:22.787 --> 00:14:25.257
I interviewed a teacher of
these three Hernandez boys

00:14:25.257 --> 00:14:27.187
that I mentioned to you and she told me

00:14:27.187 --> 00:14:30.987
that her main concern
was not the fact that

00:14:30.987 --> 00:14:33.337
they might stay behind she
says they're smart kids,

00:14:33.337 --> 00:14:35.667
they're young enough where
they will eventually catch up.

00:14:35.667 --> 00:14:37.467
She was worried about their security.

00:14:37.467 --> 00:14:40.907
She was worried that if
they're not in school

00:14:40.907 --> 00:14:42.987
that maybe they live in areas

00:14:42.987 --> 00:14:45.027
that might be a little dangerous

00:14:45.027 --> 00:14:46.827
and they go out on the streets

00:14:46.827 --> 00:14:48.327
and put themselves in danger

00:14:48.327 --> 00:14:50.477
or that because the parents go home,

00:14:50.477 --> 00:14:53.927
the older kids have to take
care of their younger siblings.

00:14:53.927 --> 00:14:58.047
Therefore don't have the
ability of spending enough time

00:14:58.047 --> 00:15:00.717
and having enough attention to the classes

00:15:00.717 --> 00:15:02.117
that are being given online.

00:15:03.177 --> 00:15:04.977
- Yeah, it's a super tough situation.

00:15:04.977 --> 00:15:08.507
We had Dr. Fauci on a couple
of couple of weeks ago,

00:15:08.507 --> 00:15:12.147
and when we played out
how this all might work

00:15:12.147 --> 00:15:13.787
and I've talked to other folks who are

00:15:13.787 --> 00:15:17.707
very well steeped in the
epidemiology of the pandemic.

00:15:17.707 --> 00:15:19.767
It looks like the best
case scenario is that

00:15:19.767 --> 00:15:22.597
we're looking at a unusual
circumstance that we're in

00:15:22.597 --> 00:15:26.137
maybe for another nine,
10, 12, the next year.

00:15:26.137 --> 00:15:27.367
And there's even some probability

00:15:27.367 --> 00:15:29.027
that goes beyond the next year.

00:15:29.027 --> 00:15:31.107
If some of these early
vaccines don't work out.

00:15:31.107 --> 00:15:32.887
And so given that, let's
just say we're looking at

00:15:32.887 --> 00:15:36.477
a year of a health crisis,

00:15:36.477 --> 00:15:38.777
a year of a lot of service industries

00:15:38.777 --> 00:15:43.307
being really difficult,
restaurant, transportation, travel,

00:15:43.307 --> 00:15:47.117
and a year of some form of
distance or hybrid learning.

00:15:47.117 --> 00:15:50.477
where do you think this is gonna leave us

00:15:50.477 --> 00:15:52.527
a year from now for having
the same conversation?

00:15:52.527 --> 00:15:54.757
What do you think is
gonna happen to society?

00:15:54.757 --> 00:15:56.257
And especially a lot of the populations,

00:15:56.257 --> 00:15:58.637
a lot of the folks that
you've been working with

00:15:58.637 --> 00:16:00.260
over the last many decades.

00:16:02.675 --> 00:16:05.287
- I think they're gonna
be hurting very much.

00:16:05.287 --> 00:16:08.058
People keep saying, we're
all in this together

00:16:08.058 --> 00:16:09.127
and we're all in the same boat,

00:16:09.127 --> 00:16:10.907
but we're not, it's the same storm,

00:16:10.907 --> 00:16:12.457
but we're in different boats.

00:16:12.457 --> 00:16:15.217
Some are in yachts,
others are in sailboats,

00:16:15.217 --> 00:16:18.717
and others are in rickety, little boats

00:16:18.717 --> 00:16:20.317
that can easily turn over.

00:16:20.317 --> 00:16:22.097
And we have to take into consideration

00:16:22.097 --> 00:16:24.737
that not everyone can survive this.

00:16:24.737 --> 00:16:28.957
Not everyone can have
homeschooling or special tutors.

00:16:28.957 --> 00:16:31.737
So it affects everyone,
we're talking mostly

00:16:31.737 --> 00:16:33.127
about kids right now,

00:16:33.127 --> 00:16:35.547
but when you talk about businesses

00:16:35.547 --> 00:16:37.687
that will not be able to survive,

00:16:37.687 --> 00:16:40.757
families that will not
be able to pay the rent.

00:16:40.757 --> 00:16:43.727
Even though there was
a moratorium on rent,

00:16:43.727 --> 00:16:46.207
there's so many families
that can become homeless,

00:16:46.207 --> 00:16:50.617
if that moratorium doesn't
extend for several more months

00:16:50.617 --> 00:16:53.627
or whatever is needed in
some States like California,

00:16:53.627 --> 00:16:57.257
that has been extended by the
governor, but it can happen.

00:16:57.257 --> 00:16:59.237
And you also have to
think of the landlords.

00:16:59.237 --> 00:17:01.787
Well, they're paying that also,

00:17:01.787 --> 00:17:04.267
there's some landlords that
might have multiple homes,

00:17:04.267 --> 00:17:06.837
and there's some that I be depending only

00:17:06.837 --> 00:17:09.477
on the rent of that person

00:17:09.477 --> 00:17:11.737
in order to be able to
pay their own bills.

00:17:11.737 --> 00:17:14.887
So it's a domino effect
that doesn't have to be,

00:17:14.887 --> 00:17:17.757
and I think that's what's
the most frustrating thing

00:17:17.757 --> 00:17:22.307
if everyone just cooperated and
everyone followed the rules,

00:17:22.307 --> 00:17:25.707
this would be over so much faster.

00:17:25.707 --> 00:17:28.458
We see the New Zealand,
was in the countries

00:17:28.458 --> 00:17:33.458
that took action early on
and didn't open up too fast.

00:17:33.487 --> 00:17:35.697
And also I think another
problem that we have here

00:17:35.697 --> 00:17:38.430
in the United States is
that there's no uniformity.

00:17:39.617 --> 00:17:43.897
It's not only that the federal
government doesn't have

00:17:43.897 --> 00:17:46.897
any specific regulations or mandates

00:17:46.897 --> 00:17:49.647
it's that they vary state by state,

00:17:49.647 --> 00:17:53.557
and even within the state,
they vary county by county.

00:17:53.557 --> 00:17:54.977
So what do you do?

00:17:54.977 --> 00:17:57.387
I mean, how do you solve a problem

00:17:57.387 --> 00:18:01.727
when it might be better
in the area where you live

00:18:01.727 --> 00:18:05.347
and maybe your neighboring
area is much worse

00:18:05.347 --> 00:18:07.627
or maybe better do you move there

00:18:07.627 --> 00:18:10.200
and create another housing crisis?

00:18:11.557 --> 00:18:14.037
I do see it as a major
problem and you're right,

00:18:14.037 --> 00:18:16.457
it could go on a lot longer.

00:18:16.457 --> 00:18:18.357
I know my daughters and I
who are working from home,

00:18:18.357 --> 00:18:23.357
they're 23 and 25 and we've
realized that the rent,

00:18:23.667 --> 00:18:26.087
we have to think that at
least the rest of this year,

00:18:26.087 --> 00:18:27.307
this is the way it's gonna be.

00:18:27.307 --> 00:18:29.437
Instead of worrying
every day and every week,

00:18:29.437 --> 00:18:30.397
is this gonna be over?

00:18:30.397 --> 00:18:32.157
Do we have, is it gonna be next week

00:18:32.157 --> 00:18:34.237
or the week after the month after

00:18:34.237 --> 00:18:36.027
just assume that the rest of the year,

00:18:36.027 --> 00:18:38.860
this is the way that we're going to live.

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:40.757
But it could happen beyond that.

00:18:40.757 --> 00:18:43.477
Like you say, it could very
well happen beyond that.

00:18:43.477 --> 00:18:44.997
And we have to prepare for this.

00:18:44.997 --> 00:18:47.697
I think it's gonna take a
catastrophe to shake us up

00:18:47.697 --> 00:18:49.717
and say, okay, we've gotta do something.

00:18:49.717 --> 00:18:52.847
Maybe it's gonna take a
complete shutdown of the country

00:18:52.847 --> 00:18:56.017
in order for us, God
willing know mark some wood

00:18:56.017 --> 00:18:58.077
knock on wood, let's
hope that doesn't happen,

00:18:58.077 --> 00:19:01.617
but if it's necessary
then it should happen

00:19:01.617 --> 00:19:04.367
because we can't go on like this.

00:19:04.367 --> 00:19:07.327
It's just hurting everyone,

00:19:07.327 --> 00:19:09.997
every single person is being hurt by this,

00:19:09.997 --> 00:19:12.587
especially low income families.

00:19:12.587 --> 00:19:15.217
- Absolutely right, and it's
definitely there's levers

00:19:15.217 --> 00:19:17.787
that are out of your mind controls.

00:19:17.787 --> 00:19:20.977
But as you imagine, the
federal and the state level

00:19:20.977 --> 00:19:24.317
pandemic response, we have
patterns other countries

00:19:24.317 --> 00:19:25.977
quicker testing, et cetera.

00:19:25.977 --> 00:19:28.447
And then what are the economic safeguards,

00:19:28.447 --> 00:19:31.187
which it does, you bring up a good point.

00:19:31.187 --> 00:19:34.297
It's not just a moratorium
on evictions or rent,

00:19:34.297 --> 00:19:36.787
the landlords, some of them
are also very vulnerable.

00:19:36.787 --> 00:19:38.577
So you have to go through
the whole system all the way

00:19:38.577 --> 00:19:40.747
through the banks and the financing system

00:19:40.747 --> 00:19:41.917
and whatever else.

00:19:41.917 --> 00:19:44.767
I'm getting a ton of questions
here on social media.

00:19:44.767 --> 00:19:47.637
This first one from Chris
Young from Facebook.

00:19:47.637 --> 00:19:50.187
He's a high school teacher new to ESL,

00:19:50.187 --> 00:19:52.287
new to English as second language,

00:19:52.287 --> 00:19:54.767
he's asking what are my must do's

00:19:54.767 --> 00:19:58.480
to be better at including
my Hispanic community.

00:19:59.707 --> 00:20:02.307
- Well, yeah, I'm glad
that you brought that up

00:20:02.307 --> 00:20:06.327
because some of the most
vulnerable are also kids

00:20:06.327 --> 00:20:08.097
that are taking ESL classes

00:20:08.097 --> 00:20:10.040
and kids that have special needs.

00:20:10.887 --> 00:20:12.217
How do you get to them?

00:20:12.217 --> 00:20:16.037
I mean, without that personal contact,

00:20:16.037 --> 00:20:17.507
what do you tell this teacher?

00:20:17.507 --> 00:20:22.227
The most important thing
is, even if they have to be,

00:20:22.227 --> 00:20:24.997
if there's to be less hours of learning,

00:20:24.997 --> 00:20:27.337
there needs to be consistency.

00:20:27.337 --> 00:20:30.417
The kids need to feel that
it's something important.

00:20:30.417 --> 00:20:32.987
They need to feel that they're
doing something important

00:20:32.987 --> 00:20:35.337
and that it's making a difference.

00:20:35.337 --> 00:20:37.127
So I think that consistency

00:20:38.037 --> 00:20:41.867
is gonna help them understand
not to get discouraged.

00:20:41.867 --> 00:20:44.680
It might take a lot
longer for them to learn,

00:20:45.807 --> 00:20:47.667
but it's better to take longer

00:20:47.667 --> 00:20:49.697
than for them not to be learning at all.

00:20:49.697 --> 00:20:54.457
So whatever ESL teachers can do,

00:20:54.457 --> 00:20:58.377
make an extra effort because
they have extra needs

00:20:58.377 --> 00:21:02.687
to teach these kids online
and to keep them engaged

00:21:02.687 --> 00:21:06.927
and make them interested in understanding

00:21:06.927 --> 00:21:11.067
that it's important for them
to do this every single day.

00:21:11.067 --> 00:21:13.387
Even if it's on weekends, why not.

00:21:13.387 --> 00:21:15.317
- Yeah, to actually stay engaged,

00:21:15.317 --> 00:21:16.647
it seems like the name
of the game right now

00:21:16.647 --> 00:21:18.297
and make sure they have
not just the academic,

00:21:18.297 --> 00:21:19.937
the social engagement.

00:21:19.937 --> 00:21:20.846
There's a question here,

00:21:20.846 --> 00:21:22.617
this is actually a really
interesting question

00:21:22.617 --> 00:21:24.007
and I'll broaden a little bit,

00:21:24.007 --> 00:21:25.727
from YouTube Dave Hankin says,

00:21:25.727 --> 00:21:27.707
Maria as a journalist
reporting on what's going on.

00:21:27.707 --> 00:21:29.067
How do you feel about the misinformation

00:21:29.067 --> 00:21:30.547
that's being spread about COVID

00:21:30.547 --> 00:21:33.177
that's not necessarily
based on science or reality.

00:21:33.177 --> 00:21:35.157
I have noticed that many
people are still in denial

00:21:35.157 --> 00:21:37.577
about how serious it really
is and will continue to be.

00:21:37.577 --> 00:21:41.407
And I'll broaden that question
even broader than COVID,

00:21:41.407 --> 00:21:44.107
because it seems like the nature of media

00:21:44.107 --> 00:21:46.667
has somewhat changed
over the last few decades

00:21:46.667 --> 00:21:48.837
where it feels like it's more polarized

00:21:48.837 --> 00:21:51.047
that people talk a lot about social media,

00:21:51.047 --> 00:21:52.667
where you can kinda be in your own bubble

00:21:52.667 --> 00:21:54.647
and only kinda hear what you wanna hear,

00:21:54.647 --> 00:21:56.227
but it also feels like that has happened

00:21:56.227 --> 00:21:57.417
even in the mainstream media

00:21:57.417 --> 00:22:00.427
where you can watch the channels

00:22:00.427 --> 00:22:02.537
that kind of feed your own viewpoint.

00:22:02.537 --> 00:22:05.087
And I I've at least
read things in the media

00:22:05.087 --> 00:22:07.787
that some of the incentives
even in the media houses

00:22:07.787 --> 00:22:09.147
seem to be much more focused about

00:22:09.147 --> 00:22:12.047
just purely engaging folks
and getting them hooked

00:22:12.047 --> 00:22:15.507
versus giving the middle
of the road information

00:22:15.507 --> 00:22:17.677
that may not necessarily
be what they wanna hear,

00:22:17.677 --> 00:22:20.497
but it was actually closer to the truth.

00:22:20.497 --> 00:22:21.330
How do you view that?

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:22.587
How do you view the evolution of media?

00:22:22.587 --> 00:22:24.059
Are you seeing the same types of trends

00:22:24.059 --> 00:22:26.107
and how do you think we can get to a world

00:22:26.107 --> 00:22:28.527
where the media is playing that role

00:22:28.527 --> 00:22:30.127
as at forth of state the institution

00:22:30.127 --> 00:22:32.527
that kinda make sure that
we all have good information

00:22:32.527 --> 00:22:34.557
and holds us accountable.

00:22:34.557 --> 00:22:37.657
- Exactly, that's the role of
the media to seek the truth,

00:22:37.657 --> 00:22:42.637
to denounce injustice, and corruption,

00:22:42.637 --> 00:22:45.787
yet we are being threatened

00:22:45.787 --> 00:22:49.107
and I think it also threatens democracy.

00:22:49.107 --> 00:22:52.040
I mean, the media is
a pillar of democracy.

00:22:52.977 --> 00:22:55.527
It's one of the pillars of
democracy, I should say.

00:22:56.557 --> 00:22:57.627
I think it's very dangerous

00:22:57.627 --> 00:22:59.217
when people don't believe the media.

00:22:59.217 --> 00:23:00.927
And I understand what you're saying,

00:23:00.927 --> 00:23:03.207
that the media is also divided,

00:23:03.207 --> 00:23:05.887
and I think this is especially true

00:23:05.887 --> 00:23:09.617
when you have these 24 hour news channels,

00:23:09.617 --> 00:23:13.967
where they have 24 hours to
fill with all this information.

00:23:13.967 --> 00:23:16.917
And also the trend that
started a few years ago

00:23:16.917 --> 00:23:20.557
of not only journalists presenting shows

00:23:20.557 --> 00:23:24.327
on these 24 hour show networks,

00:23:24.327 --> 00:23:27.857
but also people that are not journalists,

00:23:27.857 --> 00:23:29.877
people that are commentators,

00:23:29.877 --> 00:23:32.287
people that are celebrities
or commentators,

00:23:32.287 --> 00:23:33.307
they're not journalists.

00:23:33.307 --> 00:23:37.117
So if you see someone
sitting behind a desk reading

00:23:37.117 --> 00:23:38.987
with graphics on the side to you,

00:23:38.987 --> 00:23:42.047
they look like any anchor, any
journalist that is reporting,

00:23:42.047 --> 00:23:45.677
and it's not necessarily so,
they are doing commentary,

00:23:45.677 --> 00:23:48.297
they're giving their opinion,

00:23:48.297 --> 00:23:50.207
which is something that
a journalist doesn't do.

00:23:50.207 --> 00:23:52.957
But if you watch ABC, CBS, and NBC News,

00:23:52.957 --> 00:23:54.747
you're gonna see something very different.

00:23:54.747 --> 00:23:57.657
I think that's where you get
most of your information,

00:23:57.657 --> 00:24:00.747
but then you have half an hour only

00:24:00.747 --> 00:24:02.427
and out of that half an hour,

00:24:02.427 --> 00:24:04.527
it's really only 22 minutes of information

00:24:05.417 --> 00:24:08.277
of the whole world because
of the commercial breaks.

00:24:08.277 --> 00:24:10.487
So there's very little time for that.

00:24:10.487 --> 00:24:15.487
So I think really people
need to do their research.

00:24:16.517 --> 00:24:19.247
They need to, that's
their responsibility also

00:24:19.247 --> 00:24:22.041
to be out there, to read as many

00:24:22.041 --> 00:24:24.840
different sources as they can.

00:24:26.447 --> 00:24:28.877
I know in my social
media, some people say,

00:24:28.877 --> 00:24:31.067
Oh, I'm not gonna listen to the media,

00:24:31.067 --> 00:24:33.227
I'm gonna listen to the experts.

00:24:33.227 --> 00:24:35.177
And then you think, okay,
how are they gonna listen

00:24:35.177 --> 00:24:37.437
to the experts if it's
not through the media?

00:24:37.437 --> 00:24:39.297
Because the expert is
not gonna call you up

00:24:39.297 --> 00:24:41.717
or send you an email and tell you,

00:24:41.717 --> 00:24:43.577
this is the way things are.

00:24:43.577 --> 00:24:46.147
What an expert is gonna do
is have a press conference

00:24:46.147 --> 00:24:49.237
and make sure that the
media has the information

00:24:49.237 --> 00:24:51.987
that they can relay to the people,

00:24:51.987 --> 00:24:55.417
but to those affected.

00:24:55.417 --> 00:24:57.897
But it does worry me, all
these conspiracy theories.

00:24:57.897 --> 00:25:00.777
I mean, you see very
smart, intelligent people

00:25:00.777 --> 00:25:03.487
believing some of these
conspiracy theories.

00:25:03.487 --> 00:25:05.127
And I think it happens all the time.

00:25:05.127 --> 00:25:08.927
We have 350 million, I
think people in the country

00:25:08.927 --> 00:25:12.917
and we have 160 something
thousand that have died.

00:25:12.917 --> 00:25:15.397
So if it doesn't, so the
great majority of people

00:25:15.397 --> 00:25:16.840
are not affected by this.

00:25:17.787 --> 00:25:21.187
They don't maybe not know someone

00:25:21.187 --> 00:25:24.547
who has gotten sick or who has died,

00:25:24.547 --> 00:25:26.087
so if it doesn't affect
you, you don't believe it,

00:25:26.087 --> 00:25:29.107
but the moment it does,
the moment you have someone

00:25:29.107 --> 00:25:32.497
in your family getting sick or dying,

00:25:32.497 --> 00:25:36.090
that's what it takes for
you to take this seriously.

00:25:37.189 --> 00:25:39.447
And I don't know that there's
anything we can do about that,

00:25:39.447 --> 00:25:42.587
honestly, I wish there was a response.

00:25:42.587 --> 00:25:45.367
I wish I could tell you,
this is the secret sauce.

00:25:45.367 --> 00:25:48.697
This is the secret to
everybody paying attention

00:25:48.697 --> 00:25:50.587
and listening and believing,

00:25:50.587 --> 00:25:51.637
but that's not gonna happen.

00:25:51.637 --> 00:25:55.527
People have these conspiracy theories

00:25:55.527 --> 00:25:56.867
and they're gonna continue to have

00:25:56.867 --> 00:26:00.917
these conspiracy theories, no
matter how much you tell them

00:26:00.917 --> 00:26:02.757
that it's not true, but
it's very dangerous.

00:26:02.757 --> 00:26:05.227
It's very, very dangerous.

00:26:05.227 --> 00:26:07.507
- I mean to your point, something
like the crisis we're in,

00:26:07.507 --> 00:26:09.417
it might be like that type of reality,

00:26:09.417 --> 00:26:11.697
where at some point, those
opinions don't matter anymore

00:26:11.697 --> 00:26:13.937
because at some point you do know folks.

00:26:13.937 --> 00:26:15.707
I mean, just in our broader friend circle,

00:26:15.707 --> 00:26:18.157
we know at least two people
who've actually passed away

00:26:18.157 --> 00:26:22.237
from COVID and so I'm
guessing it is going to start

00:26:22.237 --> 00:26:24.287
hitting a lot more folks.

00:26:24.287 --> 00:26:26.357
I mean, related to that, have you seen,

00:26:26.357 --> 00:26:27.837
like, when you talk to journalists,

00:26:27.837 --> 00:26:30.247
people you've been
working with for decades,

00:26:30.247 --> 00:26:34.187
do they feel like, yeah,
it's the good old days,

00:26:34.187 --> 00:26:36.287
the good old days, we were all about

00:26:36.287 --> 00:26:37.597
getting the information out there,

00:26:37.597 --> 00:26:40.847
we weren't as concerned
with hooking people in.

00:26:40.847 --> 00:26:43.117
We could, it wasn't just about soundbites

00:26:43.117 --> 00:26:45.077
while now it's much shorter,

00:26:45.077 --> 00:26:47.367
it's all about grabbing
attention, is there that trend?

00:26:47.367 --> 00:26:49.707
I mean, that's obviously
I've asked that in a somewhat

00:26:49.707 --> 00:26:51.847
biased way that's at least my perception

00:26:51.847 --> 00:26:53.197
of how things have evolved.

00:26:55.477 --> 00:26:56.887
- Well, in local news,

00:26:56.887 --> 00:26:59.554
there's this saying that
"If it bleeds, it leads,"

00:27:00.607 --> 00:27:02.947
which means that yeah, most media,

00:27:02.947 --> 00:27:05.287
especially local media
always looks at something

00:27:05.287 --> 00:27:06.187
that can grab you. Why?

00:27:06.187 --> 00:27:09.377
Because people have the option of watching

00:27:09.377 --> 00:27:12.687
different channels and you wanna grab them

00:27:12.687 --> 00:27:13.520
from the beginning.

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:17.847
And that just goes to tell you
that people are very curious.

00:27:17.847 --> 00:27:19.587
I don't wanna use the word morbid,

00:27:19.587 --> 00:27:21.914
but usually those that
are more sensationalists

00:27:21.914 --> 00:27:24.167
are the ones that have
the highest ratings,

00:27:24.167 --> 00:27:25.527
people wanna watch.

00:27:25.527 --> 00:27:27.387
And then they say, I don't
wanna know, I don't wanna know,

00:27:27.387 --> 00:27:31.247
but I'm watching, I mean,
that happens all the time.

00:27:31.247 --> 00:27:34.277
I do think that we wish we
were in the good old days

00:27:34.277 --> 00:27:36.147
where we were reporting news

00:27:36.147 --> 00:27:41.017
and not being questioned the
way that we are questioned now.

00:27:41.017 --> 00:27:43.617
I'll tell you, one thing
that really affects people,

00:27:43.617 --> 00:27:48.097
is the rhetoric against the
media, mainstream media,

00:27:48.097 --> 00:27:52.017
coming from the highest
levels of government.

00:27:52.017 --> 00:27:55.256
And I worked in Spanish
language media for decades,

00:27:55.256 --> 00:27:57.607
like you mentioned in the beginning,

00:27:57.607 --> 00:28:02.087
and we know we've covered a lot
of authoritarian governments

00:28:02.087 --> 00:28:03.357
in Latin America.

00:28:03.357 --> 00:28:05.457
And we know that
authoritarian governments,

00:28:05.457 --> 00:28:07.907
what they do is one of
the first things they do

00:28:07.907 --> 00:28:10.337
is try to silence the media.

00:28:10.337 --> 00:28:14.037
They either kill them for
example, like in Khashoggi case

00:28:14.037 --> 00:28:19.037
or they close down their business,
or they put them in jail.

00:28:20.587 --> 00:28:23.537
They do anything that
they can to silence them.

00:28:23.537 --> 00:28:25.257
I know recently I was in Nicaragua

00:28:25.257 --> 00:28:27.417
and the way they tried to
silence the media there

00:28:27.417 --> 00:28:31.027
is by kind of forcing them to go digital

00:28:31.027 --> 00:28:36.027
because they put a special
tax on importing the products

00:28:37.027 --> 00:28:40.377
to publish a newspaper, the
paper, the ink, the equipment,

00:28:40.377 --> 00:28:43.077
and then another way of
doing it is calling them

00:28:44.007 --> 00:28:45.137
the enemy of the people.

00:28:45.137 --> 00:28:48.427
It's by discrediting journalists.

00:28:48.427 --> 00:28:50.117
I mean, discrediting journalists,

00:28:50.117 --> 00:28:54.157
I sometimes watch these 24 hour news

00:28:54.157 --> 00:28:56.577
lets say I watch CNN quite a bit.

00:28:56.577 --> 00:28:59.137
And I'm astonished when I
see some of these journalists

00:28:59.137 --> 00:29:00.927
that I've been watching for years,

00:29:00.927 --> 00:29:03.887
that I know are very good
journalists that I know are,

00:29:03.887 --> 00:29:04.867
I shouldn't say objective

00:29:04.867 --> 00:29:06.777
'cause objectivity doesn't exist anymore.

00:29:06.777 --> 00:29:09.837
That are balanced, that give perspective

00:29:09.837 --> 00:29:12.207
because that's what journalists
are supposed to be doing.

00:29:12.207 --> 00:29:15.637
Constantly fact checking,
they're spending their whole time

00:29:15.637 --> 00:29:18.357
on the air, fact checking
fact checking, fact checking,

00:29:18.357 --> 00:29:21.547
fact checking and playing devil's advocate

00:29:21.547 --> 00:29:25.277
against the nonbelievers.

00:29:25.277 --> 00:29:29.357
And I think what's happening
now has pushed people

00:29:29.357 --> 00:29:30.380
to be that way.

00:29:32.357 --> 00:29:35.030
But I still think it's a noble profession.

00:29:36.987 --> 00:29:40.587
Still very honored to be a journalist.

00:29:40.587 --> 00:29:43.187
And I still do believe that our profession

00:29:43.187 --> 00:29:46.427
is a profession that
we need to be proud of

00:29:46.427 --> 00:29:49.597
and that we are there to serve the public.

00:29:49.597 --> 00:29:54.097
We are there, we are the only
barrier between those in power

00:29:54.097 --> 00:29:56.940
and those that they are trying to reach.

00:29:58.547 --> 00:30:00.377
So if we weren't so important

00:30:00.377 --> 00:30:02.800
they wouldn't be trying to silence us.

00:30:04.035 --> 00:30:06.707
- Completely agree and as you point out,

00:30:06.707 --> 00:30:09.097
now with social media where
anyone can publish anything

00:30:09.097 --> 00:30:12.627
to have professionals who
are really going after facts,

00:30:12.627 --> 00:30:14.507
I think couldn't be more important

00:30:14.507 --> 00:30:16.557
than any other point in history.

00:30:16.557 --> 00:30:18.177
Maria, whenever we're having

00:30:18.177 --> 00:30:19.267
these interesting conversations,

00:30:19.267 --> 00:30:22.187
the time goes way faster
than I ever expect.

00:30:22.187 --> 00:30:24.337
I can talk to you for hours,

00:30:24.337 --> 00:30:25.600
but I just really wanted
to thank you on my behalf

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:27.417
and whole the Khan Academy team

00:30:27.417 --> 00:30:28.837
and all the viewers we
have for joining us.

00:30:28.837 --> 00:30:31.687
I think we learned a lot and
I hope we can do this again.

00:30:33.097 --> 00:30:34.127
- Thank you so much, Sal,

00:30:34.127 --> 00:30:35.857
I really appreciate the opportunity,

00:30:35.857 --> 00:30:37.857
sorry, sometimes I tend to talk too much

00:30:37.857 --> 00:30:40.627
and go on and on and
on, but these are issues

00:30:40.627 --> 00:30:43.197
that I'm passionate about,
I know that you are too,

00:30:43.197 --> 00:30:46.367
and I thank you on behalf
of the Latino community

00:30:46.367 --> 00:30:49.517
for also having this
(speaking in foreign language)

00:30:49.517 --> 00:30:54.517
and making sure that students
of different nationalities

00:30:54.637 --> 00:30:59.017
and the country and the world
have some kind of support

00:30:59.017 --> 00:31:02.047
so that they can continue
so they can get ahead.

00:31:02.047 --> 00:31:03.847
Education is one of the
most important things

00:31:03.847 --> 00:31:05.347
that we can have.

00:31:05.347 --> 00:31:09.604
And I think that everyone
is very well aware of that.

00:31:09.604 --> 00:31:12.687
And in our case, in the Latino community,

00:31:12.687 --> 00:31:16.747
immigrant families know that
education is the only way

00:31:16.747 --> 00:31:19.317
their kids won't have to go
through what they went through

00:31:19.317 --> 00:31:20.217
in order to get ahead.

00:31:20.217 --> 00:31:22.847
So thank you so much for what you do.

00:31:22.847 --> 00:31:23.680
- Thank you.

00:31:25.327 --> 00:31:27.007
Well, everyone thanks so much

00:31:27.007 --> 00:31:28.607
for joining today's live stream,

00:31:29.787 --> 00:31:32.504
every time I think we're
like 10 minutes into it

00:31:32.504 --> 00:31:34.887
and I realized I'm 31 minutes into it.

00:31:34.887 --> 00:31:36.947
'Cause there's so much to talk about,

00:31:36.947 --> 00:31:39.547
but I hopefully you enjoy the
conversation today with Maria,

00:31:39.547 --> 00:31:40.380
as much as I did.

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:43.747
I look forward to more
of these conversations,

00:31:43.747 --> 00:31:47.487
we're going to have a
Lester Holt on tomorrow,

00:31:47.487 --> 00:31:49.787
and I think we'll actually
be able to continue

00:31:49.787 --> 00:31:52.487
to some degree this
conversation about the media

00:31:52.487 --> 00:31:54.517
and also I always find it interesting

00:31:54.517 --> 00:31:58.117
because journalists report on
what's going on in the world.

00:31:58.117 --> 00:31:59.737
But these are also very thoughtful folks

00:31:59.737 --> 00:32:03.167
who are seeing aspects
of society in the world

00:32:03.167 --> 00:32:04.897
that many of us don't get to see.

00:32:04.897 --> 00:32:06.217
And so I think they really form

00:32:06.217 --> 00:32:08.197
really interesting perspectives about

00:32:08.197 --> 00:32:11.597
the context that we're in and
maybe ways to get out of them

00:32:11.597 --> 00:32:12.777
or make them better.

00:32:12.777 --> 00:32:14.737
But once again, thanks so much for joining

00:32:14.737 --> 00:32:16.670
and I will see y'all tomorrow.

